 COUNT(*) AS underfunded_goal_count\n  FROM ATOM_ENTITY_INVESTMENT_GOAL_001\n  WHERE shortfall > 0\n  GROUP BY investor_id\n  HAVING COUNT(*) >= 1\n)\nSELECT \n  mi.investor_id,\n  mi.investor_name\nFROM moderate_investors mi\nINNER JOIN portfolio_health_check ph ON mi.investor_id = ph.investor_id\nINNER JOIN scenario_count sc ON mi.investor_id = sc.investor_id\nINNER JOIN goal_count gc ON mi.investor_id = gc.investor_id"", ""generate_sql_reasoning"": ""The query uses two-level aggregation to handle one-to-many relationships without fanout distortion. First, CTEs pre-aggregate each condition at the investor_id level: (1) moderate_investors filters the base table for 'Moderate' risk tolerance, (2) portfolio_health_check identifies investors with any diversification_score < 25 record using DISTINCT, (3) scenario_count counts Economic Growth scenarios per investor and filters for >=1 with HAVING, (4) goal_count counts underfunded goals per investor and filters for >=1 with HAVING. The final SELECT INNER JOINs all CTEs on investor_id to intersect the sets, ensuring investors meet all four conditions. Output schema projects investor_id and investor_name from the base table."", ""generate_sql_parse_error"": """", ""generate_sql_raw_response_preview"": "" {\n  \""sql\"": \""WITH \\nmoderate_investors AS (\\n  SELECT investor_id, investor_name\\n  FROM ATOM_ENTITY_INVESTOR_PROFILE_001\\n  WHERE risk_tolerance = 'Moderate'\\n),\\nportfolio_health_check AS (\\n  SELECT DISTINCT investor_id\\n  FROM ATOM_ENTITY_PORTFOLIO_HEALTH_001\\n  WHERE diversification_score < 25\\n),\\nscenario_count AS (\\n  SELECT \\n    investor_id,\\n    COUNT(*) AS economic_growth_scenario_count\\n  FROM ATOM_EVENT_SCENARIO_REBALANCING_001\\n  WHERE scenario = 'Economic Growth'\\n  GROUP BY inve"", ""pre_scan_validation_is_valid"": true, ""pre_scan_validation_reason"": ""Valid"", ""scan_status"": ""success"", ""scan_row_count"": 4, ""scan_error"": """", ""scan_error_type"": """", ""scan_raw_rows"": 